## Core Design Decisions

### 1. Location = Pure Pusher Client Events (P2P, No Backend/DB)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Driver's phone sends GPS coordinates **directly through Pusher** to branch dashboard and user frontend. **Backend is NOT involved** for location updates. **No DB writes** for live location — it's ephemeral data that only matters during active delivery.

**Flow:**
```
Driver Phone → Pusher Cloud → User Browser + Branch Dashboard
(No backend hit, no DB write, no server load)
```

**Why:** Live location is ephemeral — once delivery is done, no one cares where driver was 5 minutes ago. Saving to DB would add unnecessary load for data that's never queried.

### 2. Backend Only Handles Status Changes

Backend REST API is only used for:
- Pusher auth endpoint (required for private channels, called ONCE per subscription)
- Driver login (dummy for testing)
- Delivery assignment (branch assigns driver → order)
- Status changes (accept, pickup, delivered) → saved to MongoDB + Pusher server trigger
- Vehicle management

### 3. Smart Distance-Based Filtering on Driver Phone

```
IF (moved > 50 meters from last sent position) → SEND location immediately
ELSE IF (30 seconds since last send) → SEND heartbeat
ELSE → DON'T SEND (saves Pusher messages)
```

**Result:** ~60% fewer Pusher messages compared to blind 10-second polling.

### 4. Location Tracking Lifecycle

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> OrderPlaced: User places delivery order
    OrderPlaced --> Preparing: Kitchen starts cooking
    Preparing --> Ready: Kitchen marks ready
    Ready --> DriverAssigned: Branch assigns driver
    DriverAssigned --> PickedUp: Driver picks up food
    
    PickedUp --> TrackingActive: 30 sec delay then GPS starts
    
    state TrackingActive {
        [*] --> Sending: watchPosition active
        Sending --> Sending: Smart filtered (50m/30s)
    }
    
    TrackingActive --> Delivered: Driver marks delivered
    Delivered --> TrackingStopped: GPS stops, channels cleaned up
    TrackingStopped --> [*]
```

- **GPS START:** 30 seconds after `picked-up` status
- **GPS STOP:** Immediately when `delivered` status is set
- **No tracking** when driver is `available`, `offline`, or waiting at restaurant

### 5. Driver Navigation — Native Maps Deep Link (No In-App Map)

When driver clicks the map/pin icon:
```js
// Detect platform and open native maps
const lat = customerLocation.lat;
const lng = customerLocation.lng;
const address = encodeURIComponent(customerAddress);

// Try Google Maps first (works on both Android & iOS)
const url = `https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=${lat},${lng}`;
window.open(url, '_blank');
```
- **Android:** Opens Google Maps app with route from current location → customer
- **iOS:** Opens Apple Maps or Google Maps with route
- **No in-app map needed for drivers**

### 6. Each Restaurant Has Its Own Drivers

Drivers belong to a specific restaurant. In V2 (multi-restaurant), each restaurant manages only its own drivers. Channel isolation ensures no cross-restaurant data leakage.

